+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, with no spaces, so `1h30m`, `90m`,
+and `1d2h3m4s` are valid but `30m1h` and `1h 30m` are not. Units are case-insensitive. Negative and decimal values
+are rejected. A `timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is used unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
